# To move [[swiftly]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] forward [[quickly]] upon [[two]] [[feet]] by alternately making a short [[jump]] off either foot.
##: {{cot|en|walk}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Run''', and you might still catch the train!}}
##* {{see more citations|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
## {{senseid|en|go at a fast pace}} {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[go]] at a [[fast]] pace; to move [[quickly]].
##: {{ux|en|I have been '''running''' all over the building looking for him.}}
##: {{ux|en|Sorry, I've got to '''run'''; my house is on fire!}}
##* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Ed Cobb|authorlabel=no|title=w:Tainted Love|album=w:Non-Stop Erotic Cabaret|year=1965|year_published=1981|artist=w:Soft Cell|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XZVpR3Pk-r8|text=Once I '''ran''' to you (I ran) / Now I '''run''' from you / This tainted love you've given / I give you all a boy could give you}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cover (a course or a distance) by running.
##: {{ux|en|I can '''run''' a mile, but I can't '''run''' the cross-country course.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To complete a running course or event in (a given time).
##: {{ux|en|I was hoping to make the team, but I didn't '''run''' the qualifying time.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move briskly or smoothly with a motion of sliding, rolling, sweeping etc.
##: {{ux|en|The shuttle '''runs''' back and forth on these rollers.}}
##: {{ux|en|As its name suggests, the monorail '''runs''' on a single rail.}}
##: {{ux|en|I felt her fingers '''running''' over my cheek.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause]] to move [[quickly]] or [[lightly]].
##: {{ux|en|Every day I '''run''' my dog across the field and back.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'll just '''run''' the vacuum cleaner over the carpet.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Run''' your fingers through my hair.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Run''' a flag up (the pole/mast)}}
##* {{RQ:Doyle Lost World|passage=Challenger and I '''ran''' Summerlee along, one at each of his elbows, while Lord John covered our retreat, firing again and again as savage heads snarled at us out of the bushes.}}
## {{senseid|en|race}} {{lb|en|transitive|or|intransitive}} To [[compete]] in a [[race]].
##: {{ux|en|The horse will '''run''' in the Preakness next year.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm not ready to '''run''' a marathon.}}
##: {{ux|en|The car could not be repaired in time for the race and did not '''run'''. }}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[transport]] (someone or something), notionally at a brisk pace.
##: {{ux|en|Could you '''run''' me over to the store?}}
##: {{ux|en|Please '''run''' this report upstairs to director's office.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} Of a means of transportation: to [[travel]] (a [[route]]).
##: {{ux|en|The bus (train, plane, ferry boat, etc) '''runs''' between Newport and Riverside.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Karl-Heinz Reger; Nelles Verlag Staff|title=Malaysia - Singapore - Brunei| publisher=Hunter Publishing, Inc| isbn=9783886189021| page=91| text=Small planes '''run''' between Alor and Langkawi. BUS: Express busses leave the bus terminal on the corner of Jl. Langgar and Jl. Stesyen for K. Kedah, {{nb...}}}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (a  vehicle) to  travel a route.
##: {{ux|en|Hundreds of cars '''run''' along this highway every day.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2024|month=11|journal=Lackawanna Coalition Railgram|author=David Peter Alan|title=NJ Transit Runs Special Trains to “Railroad Museum [for a day]”|url=https://lackawannacoalition.org/2024/11/25/nj-transit-runs-special-trains-to-railroad-museum-for-a-day/|text=This year, NJ Transit allowed nonmotorists {{…}} to reach the event by '''running''' special trains every 2 hours 4 round trips). The location was the old Lackawanna Railroad freight house, about a 10-minute walk from the Boonton Station, which normally has service only during peak-commuting hours on weekdays.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260510175725/https://lackawannacoalition.org/2024/11/25/nj-transit-runs-special-trains-to-railroad-museum-for-a-day/|archivedate=10 May 2026}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[transit]] (a length of a river), as in [[whitewater rafting]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1979|author=United States. Forest Service. Rocky Mountain Region|title=Piedra River: Final Environmental Impact Statement & Wild & Scenic River Study| page=74| text=To put it frankly, if you people had to hire others to run the river and survey it for you, if, in short, you can't even '''run''' it yourself, why do think you can decide who is and who is not competent? River running, as has been{{nb...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of fish, to [[migrate]] for [[spawning]].
## {{lb|en|American football|transitive|or|intransitive}} To carry (a [[football]]) down the field, as opposed to passing or kicking.
## {{senseid|en|flee}} {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[flee]] from a [[danger]] or towards help.
##: {{ux|en|Whenever things get tough, she cuts and '''runs'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|When he's broke, he '''runs''' to me for money.}}
##: {{ux|en|When the alarm went off, the thief dropped the booty and '''ran'''.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|transitive}} To [[pass]] (without [[stop]]ping), typically a stop [[signal]], [[stop sign]]{{,}} or [[duty]] to [[yield]] the [[right of way]].
##: {{ux|en|The car in front just '''ran''' a red light.}}
##: {{ux|en|If you have a collision with a vehicle oncoming from the right, after having '''run''' priority to the right, you are at fault.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|juggling|colloquial}} To juggle a pattern [[continuously]], as opposed to starting and stopping quickly.
# To flow.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a [[liquid]] or [[electric current]], to [[flow]].
